now to ethiopia where a civil war has  left tens of thousands dead and millions  displaced as government troops and the  tigran people's liberation front battle  over territory an investigation by the  united nations found all sides in the  conflict had committed violations  including using rape as a weapon of war  our reporter mariel muller went to the  amhara region to meet women recovering  from the trauma and here's a warning the  following report contains descriptions  of sexual violence  jeanette had no chance to run when four  men came to her garden  we are not identifying her in this  report  again  one of them put his feet on my shoulder  and burned me  while one of them burnt me with a  cigarette  another was forcing me to have sex with  him  the four men raped her for several hours  they left her with hiv and other  infections  and a deep fear  if they come back and take control of  the town again i have to die i'm very  scared  in this town hike during that raid last  november the men were fighters loyal to  the tigray people's liberation front or  tplf but men on both sides are using  sexual violence as a weapon of war  when janet asked the tigrans why they  were raping her  they told her government forces had done  the same in their towns to their mothers  and sisters  she's doing her best now to build a  future and has started a distance  learning course  she wants to set up a small business  i want to go to school because i didn't  attend a school as a child  now education is good for my work to get  more knowledge and live a better life  just a few streets away a 14 year old  girl she also wishes to remain anonymous  the the grain fighters came to her home  too  one of them said i will take one of you  and i'll kill one  my aunt begged him but he said no i will  kill you and take one of you  so i told him to leave them and do  whatever he wants to me  he didn't kill her he raped her for much  of the night  since then she often misses school  for medical appointments but also to  avoid gossip and bullying  i prefer not to go to school  the people i used to play with there  now when i greet them  they don't want to play with me  but like jeanette salam has plans she  intends to become a police officer or  soldier herself  and get her own form of justice  i wish i could find him and kill him i  want him to die by my hand  we've listened to several other women  with similar stories across amhara  region  they can tell the world what happened  women over the buddha in tigre have  suffered similar attacks  but the de facto ban on journalists  means their voices cannot yet be heard
 

